a1_foyer.text:
{{#if name_given}}{{#if name}}"{{name}}," your host says, once, and a coat hook stirs.{{else}}You give him an empty name. Somewhere the pen writes it down anyway.{{/if}}{{else}}He has nothing to call you, and lets that be.{{/if}}

The foyer is gold and very warm. Three portraits hang on the far wall with their eyes painted out.{{#if candle}} By the candle the painted eyes are only paint.{{/if}} Along the near wall run brass hooks tagged with years, 1898, 1931, 1953, 1979, each coat hung by someone who meant to come back. Every clock in the room says 11:59, and not one of them has moved.

Under the floor, something knocks once and stops, as if it had remembered its manners. Your host waits at the inner arch. "After you." Then, lightly: "Did anyone see you come up the path?"

a1_riddle.text:
{{#if lied}}{{#if lies=1}}"Ah," says your host.{{#if debt>=1}} He takes back the step he had taken toward you. "Then we are playing after all, and you are our visitor."{{/if}}{{/if}}{{else}}"Then you have told me no lies," says your host, as if that were the answer he wanted.{{/if}} The waltz holds one note while the house makes up your room, and he proposes a small game.{{#if stance=trick}} "A point to whoever gets it," he says.{{/if}}

"I have a face and two hands, and I have not moved since the night a boy came to the door. What am I?"{{#if visits>0}} He asks it exactly as he asked it before, and does not seem to mind that you know.{{/if}}

He waits, gloved hands folded. Around you the portraits, the coats and the clocks all hold very still, waiting to hear which of them you choose.

a2_hub.text:
{{#if riddle=right}}"The clock," your host agrees, and touches his watch chain without seeming to know he has.{{else}}Your host looks for a long moment at what you named. "A good guess," he says, which is not the same as a right one.{{/if}}

The foyer opens out now that you are in it. To the left, panelled doors and a fire being kind to itself. Down a passage, bread and cloves. Past both, a green light that comes from no lamp, the kind you could only reach through another room. The stair climbs into the dark behind a velvet rope.

"You will not have time for all of them," he says, quite plainly.{{#if visits>0}} You never have.{{/if}} {{#if stance=treat}}"Do say, my guest, if there is anything you need," he adds.{{else}}{{#if riddle=right}}"A point to our visitor, for the clock," he adds, keeping score to the chandelier.{{else}}"One to the house, for the riddle," he adds, keeping score to the chandelier.{{/if}}{{/if}}

a2_hub_stair.text:
{{#if ledger}}{{#if wishbone}}{{#if knocks>=1}}Behind you the kitchen floor says one word, in Hester's voice: "Welcome." A spoon drops, and nobody picks it up.{{else}}Behind you the kitchen has gone quiet, and the warm flagstone has settled back level with the rest.{{/if}}{{else}}Your host stands at the conservatory sill with his pen uncapped, and writes nothing. You gave him nothing to write.{{/if}}{{else}}Your host stands at the conservatory sill with his pen uncapped, and writes nothing. You gave him nothing to write.{{/if}}

You come back to a foyer with one door fewer. {{#if ledger}}{{#if wishbone}}The green light is gone.{{else}}The bread smell has gone out of the air.{{/if}}{{else}}There is no firelight to the left any more.{{/if}} Where its door stood there is wallpaper, still a little damp.{{#if candle}} By your candle the door still shows through.{{/if}} The velvet rope is down, and the stair climbs into the dark.

{{#if stance=treat}}{{#if candle}}Your host glances at your candle and offers you his arm all the same.{{else}}Your host offers you his arm.{{/if}} "It is dark on the stair, my guest."{{else}}Your host crooks his arm at you with a little bow, a move in the game. "Our visitor may take it, of course. I think that would be a point to the house."{{/if}}

a2_kitchen_thanks.text:
"Thank you, Hester," you say.

She stops wiping her hands. Ninety-five years, and nobody has said it. She does not cry. {{#if stance=treat}}She takes the wishbone off its saucer by the range and presses it into your palm, small and clean and forked.{{else}}Without turning round she reaches behind her, takes the wishbone off its saucer by the range, and presses it into your palm.{{/if}} "That's not on his books," she says. "A thank-you isn't."

Nowhere in the house does a pen lift. {{#if stance=treat}}Then, because she cannot help it, she is already reaching for a plate. "You'll have a little something, though."{{else}}"I still don't serve strangers," she says, and leaves the lid off the pot.{{/if}}

a2_cons_door.text:
{{#if knocks>=1}}As you leave the kitchen, the floor says "Welcome" in the cook's voice. The cook is standing at the range with her mouth shut.{{else}}{{#unless ledger}}As you leave the kitchen, the floor stays quiet, and behind you the cook lets out a breath.{{/unless}}{{/if}}

Behind a curtain of ivy, glass, and behind the glass, green. Vines press flat to the panes and lean toward your warmth. Far back sits a boy in a skeleton suit whose bones stopped glowing in 1979, a candle in a tin dish on his knee. "Dez," he says, and taps the dish twice. "House can't quite get in here. Too much glass."

{{#if stance=treat}}{{#if fed}}He smells the cloves on you. "Aw, man. You ate." He holds it out anyway. "Promise you won't again."{{else}}He looks you over. "Guest, huh. Oh, man. It's yours if you promise never to eat in this house. Not a debt. A promise."{{/if}}{{else}}He grins. "Another stranger. Outstanding. Here, man. Take it."{{#if candle}} Your flame and his bow toward each other. "Same light, man. Always was. Take mine and they're just the one."{{/if}}{{/if}}

a2_cons_lie.text:
Your host writes it down in careful capitals: ONLY VINES.{{#if lies=1}} "Ah," he says.{{#if debt>=1}} "Then we are playing after all, and you are our visitor." The arm he had half offered you, a guest's arm, goes back to his side.{{/if}}{{else}} This time he does not say "Ah." He underlines it, once for each story you have told him tonight.{{#if lies>=3}} "Our visitor is very good at this," he says, pleased.{{/if}}{{/if}}

He walks you back to a foyer with one door fewer. {{#if ledger}}The bread smell has gone out of the air.{{else}}There is no firelight to the left any more.{{/if}} Where its door stood, the paste on the wallpaper has not dried.{{#if candle}} By your candle the door still shows through.{{/if}}

The velvet rope is down. At the foot of the stair he crooks an elbow at you with a little bow. It is not a guest's arm. It is a move in the game.

a3_hall_arm.text:
Your hand is on your host's arm. Through the sleeve it is a boy's wrist in a man's cuff.{{#if stance=trick}} "And that one goes to the house," he tells the portraits, pleased.{{/if}}{{#if candle}} He keeps his face turned from your flame.{{/if}}{{#if fed}} The runner is soft underfoot, and when you look back the stair is gone, and you do not mind.{{/if}}

He walks you along until the hall is exactly the width of the house, then lets go. He does not go through doors first.

{{#if name_given}}Your name is painted on the nearer door.{{#if name}} It says {{name}}, spelled very carefully.{{/if}} Cold comes under the mirror room's door. The attic steps wait beyond, and the hall, politely, will not let you that far yet.{{else}}The nearer door has a smooth place where a handle would go. Cold comes under the mirror room's door. With one door fewer to offer, the hall lets you reach the attic steps.{{/if}}

a3_hall_2.text:
{{#if ribbon}}You go with her ribbon in your pocket and her promise in your mouth, and the nursery door closes softly behind you.{{else}}You leave her there, and the nursery door shuts behind you with a small, decided click.{{/if}}{{#if trust<=-2}} Then a key turns, on the inside.{{/if}}

The hall is shorter than it was. Cold still comes under the mirror room's door, and at the far end the attic steps stand open, going up into the sound of rain. Behind you the house is rolling the hall up like a runner after a party.

One more room tonight. The house makes that quite clear without saying anything at all.

a3_nursery_pip.text:
{{#if trust<=-1}}The sheet has gone very still where you laughed at it. Nobody else ever comes, though, so she talks to you anyway.{{else}}{{#if stance=treat}}{{#if trust>=1}}She tucks your sweet away under the sheet, quickly, before anyone can count it.{{else}}She whispers hello back, as quietly as you said it.{{/if}}{{else}}She laughs into the sheet, the muffled laugh of an accomplice.{{/if}}{{/if}} "I'm Pip," she says. "Philippa Ashdown, really, but nobody says it. Is it still Halloween out there? I said I'd be home before Mum switches the porch light off. I've only been here about an hour."

Her eyeholes go to the clock. {{#if riddle=right}}"The house says you got the clocks right. First one ever. So you'd know what year it really is."{{else}}"They all say the same. He says they're only being polite."{{/if}}{{#if visits>0}} She tilts her head. "You've the look of someone who came back. I don't know how I know that."{{/if}}

Inside the wall something knocks three times, and waits for an answer too.

a3_nursery_year.text:
You tell her. The sheet goes still, and then her fingers start moving under it, counting. She gets past her own age, and past her mother's, and stops.

"That's not an hour," she says.

She goes to the window anyway and kneels on the chair. {{#if fed}}Where she looks you see only a grey nothing, and she looks at it a long time.{{else}}At the bottom of a street that runs on too far, a porch light in a little iron cage is still on.{{/if}}

{{#if trust>=2}}She unties a ribbon from under the sheet. "Then you'd better take me. Promise properly. Promises count at midnight; everyone knows that."{{else}}{{#if trust=1}}She winds a ribbon round her fingers and does not hold it out. Not yet.{{else}}She keeps her hands on the glass and does not ask you anything else.{{/if}}{{/if}}

a3_nursery_stay.text:
You sit down on the floor by her chair, and stay. Nobody counts how long. In this house a while costs nothing on the clock: the hands do not move, however long you sit.

{{#if fed}}She watches the grey nothing beyond the glass, and you watch her.{{else}}Far down the street, the caged porch light is still on.{{/if}}

{{#if trust>=2}}At last she unties the ribbon from under the sheet. "Then you'd better take me. Promise properly. Promises count at midnight; everyone knows that."{{else}}{{#if trust>=0}}After a while the sheet leans against your shoulder, a little. The ribbon stays wound round her fingers.{{else}}She lets you sit, and says nothing to you at all.{{/if}}{{/if}}

a3_mirror_no.text:
You tell it no.

{{#if stance=treat}}"Oh," it says, in your voice, hurt. "You'd have been doing me a kindness. Only a small one."{{else}}"Your move, then," it says, in your voice, amused. "I'll wait. I'm good at waiting."{{/if}}{{#if fed}} The front door in the glass begins, very slightly, to fade.{{else}} It stays half a second late a moment longer, and then, pointedly, keeps perfect time.{{/if}}{{#if ribbon}} "She'll need someone in her chair, you know."{{/if}}

"The offer stands," it says. "Right up until you leave this room."

a3_hatch.text:
Below you, something has stopped knocking and started waiting.{{#if knocks>=2}} Answered more than once tonight, it sounds less like a knock than a conversation.{{/if}}{{#if mirror}} Behind you, in your own voice, someone is still saying "Thank you."{{/if}}

When you come out, the hall is gone. In its place a narrow back stair drops under a hatch with a brass ring, and warm air breathes up it: in, a long pause, out. The doors you did not open have become wallpaper, the pattern not quite matched at the seams.

{{#if mirror}}Nobody is in the shine of the brass ring.{{/if}}{{#if trust>=1}} At the foot of the stair a sheet with crooked eyeholes waits, pointing down.{{/if}}{{#if trust<=-2}} The hatch already stands open. Someone below knows you are coming.{{/if}}

a4_cellar.text:
The hatch comes down over your head and shuts. Pale roots come through the cellar walls, and they have pushed the bricks aside so gently that the mortar is still whole. They all lean one way. Shelves of jars run off into the dark. It smells of warm bread and cloves.{{#if fed}} You are hungry again.{{/if}}{{#if trust>=1}}

The sheet with crooked eyeholes slips past you, close to the floor. "Round the big root," she whispers. "Don't look at it."{{else}}{{#if trust<=-2}}

The roots part before you reach them. Somebody told the house which way you would come.{{/if}}{{/if}}

a4_jars_sweets.text:
You twist the lid off and take them. The sweets are warm in your hand, waxed paper twisted at both ends, never spent.{{#if stance=treat}} They are the same as the three the gloved hand gave you at the door.{{/if}}{{#if trust>=0}}

The sheet beside you does not reach for them. "I was saving those," she says. "For when I got home." Her crooked eyeholes stay on your hand.{{else}}

On the shelf the emptied jar goes on turning, slowly, looking for them. Nobody else down here minds.{{/if}}

a4_table.text:
The door opens inward, slowly, for someone expected. Beyond is the Marrow: a table under the roots, set for hundreds.{{#if knocks>=3}} Under it something has heard the count come whole, and begun to come up.{{/if}} The place cards are in careful capitals: *H. VANE. MISS P. ASHDOWN. D. OKAFOR.* Then yours.{{#if name}} It says *{{name}}*, spelled right.{{/if}}{{#if last_ending=consumed}} Its corners are soft from being set out before.{{/if}}

Mr. Marrow waits at the head. "After you."{{#unless name_given}} Your card is blank. "The house will know what to call you."{{/unless}}{{#if riddle=right}} "The clocks stopped the night I said Treat," he says. "You were first to guess."{{else}}{{#if riddle=wrong}}{{#if clock}} "Every clock says 11:59," he says, "and one of them is ticking."{{else}} "Every clock says 11:59," he says, "as it did when you came in."{{/if}}{{/if}}{{/if}}

He opens a green book. {{#if debt>=1}}{{#if debt=1}}Your page holds a single neat stroke.{{else}}{{#if debt=2}}Your page holds two neat strokes.{{else}}{{#if debt=3}}Your page holds three neat strokes.{{else}}{{#if debt=4}}Your page holds four neat strokes.{{else}}{{#if debt=5}}Your page holds five neat strokes.{{else}}{{#if debt>=6}}Your page holds six neat strokes.{{/if}}{{/if}}{{/if}}{{/if}}{{/if}}{{/if}}{{else}}Your page is blank.{{#if stance=treat}} He does not say "Lovely."{{/if}}{{/if}} A silver lid keeps your plate warm.

a4_gate_midnight.text:
The clock behind Mr. Marrow lets go of its minute hand, and strikes. Every clock in the house answers at once, after a hundred and twenty-eight years. The candles bow.

"Midnight," Mr. Marrow says between strokes, holding the back of your chair. "It lets go of every guest who owes nothing, and anyone holding a living promise to leave. It forgives nothing else."{{#if stance=trick}} He looks at you. "Guests, mind. It is particular."{{/if}}{{#if lied}} For you each stroke arrives a little late, and you find yourself counting them.{{/if}}{{#if debt>=1}} Beside your plate the green book stays open.{{/if}}

Four. Five. Six. It will only come once.

end_paid_in_full.text:
"The bill," you say. "Please."

Mr. Marrow takes a long slip from the green book and turns it round by your plate: *Oct. 31*, and ruled columns. In the column marked *Item* there is one line.{{#if name_given}} The line is your name.{{else}} It is a blank the length of a name, which is enough for it.{{/if}}{{#if debt>=1}} What you owed is listed down the side, and every line is crossed out, settled with what you were.{{else}}{{#if stance=treat}} Under *Owing* every line is struck through, or was never written. You paid your way, and that is worse: you have no claim on anything here, and the house is free to keep you.{{#unless clock}} "A clean page is let out at midnight," he says. "Midnight did not come."{{/unless}}{{else}} Under *Owing* nothing was ever written. "Our visitor owes the house nothing," he says, "and the house owes our visitor nothing. That is the trouble with it." You have no claim on anything here, and the house is free to keep you.{{/if}}{{/if}}

He closes the book and says, gently, "Lovely."{{#if name_given}} Your name goes down into the warm dark with all the others.{{else}} The blank goes down into the warm dark with all the others, and fills.{{/if}}
